# Description: Read EF.DIR, which lists the applications available on the card.
# Category: reading
#
# WHAT THIS DOES
# Step 1 selects EF.DIR (the standard application directory file) and step 2
# reads it from the beginning. EF.DIR contains a directory of the
# applications on the card: each entry is a small record whose most useful
# field is the application identifier (AID). Copy any AID you find here into
# the select_aid_template script to select that application directly.
# Reading does not modify the card.
#
# THE COMMANDS, BYTE BY BYTE
#   00 A4 02 00 02 2F 00
#       SELECT FILE, P1 = 2 (select an elementary file by identifier),
#       file identifier 2F 00 = EF.DIR.
#   00 B0 00 00 00
#       READ BINARY, offset 0 (P1-P2 form a 15-bit offset, here 0), requesting
#       256 bytes (short Le 00 means 256). The card may return fewer at EOF.
#
# WHAT YOU SHOULD SEE
#   After the SELECT: SW 90 00, or SW 61 XX with data still waiting.
#   After the READ: the EF.DIR contents followed by SW 90 00; SW 6B 00 means
#   the offset lies beyond the end of the file.
#
# These commands do not write anything to the card.
00 A4 02 00 02 2F 00
00 B0 00 00 00
